  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 AM MDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nToday through Sun night...Strong upper level flow will be \nmixing down to the surface. It should be stronger than \nyesterday. Combined with continued very low humidity over a \nslightly smaller portion of the lower elevations, Red Flag \nWarning has been re-issued for the Idaho Falls WFS area, Fire \nWeather Forecast Zone 410. The area of strongest wind shifts \nfarther southward on Saturday, and there is potential for low \nhumidity to continue there as well. The strongest wind is\nexpected Sunday, as a trough moving through over Sat night\nbrings the tightest gradient of the next three days.\n\nThe passage of the trough Sat night brings a strong cooling,\nsimilar to something seen more in a winter regime, hinting at a\ncoming transition. Afternoon highs should cool 12 to 16 deg F\nfrom Sat to Sun. \n\nThe expected rainfall amounts have collapsed to near zero for\nthis period. It is rainer this morning than any other period.\nThis was the trend yesterday with Sat returning to temperatures\nwarmer than Fri. The cool down previously from Sat to Sun was in\nexcess of 20 deg F. This is indicative of reduced cloud cover\nand moisture. \n\nMon through Thu...A lingering troughing continues on Mon, so\nthere as an additional 2 to 6 deg F cooling. Warming returns Tue\nas the Gem State sits between two highs, with a closed low\ndeveloping off the OR-WA coast. The ridges strengthen during\nthis period, thus bringing warmer temperatures, getting back\nabove normal for the time of year by Wed. It also should bring\nlighter wind as early as Mon. Near zero threat of precipitation\nor thunderstorm activity during this mid-range forecast period.",
